ANNOTATION
Subsection (1)(t)(V), the minor conversion therapy law, does not violate the free speech clause of the first amendment to the federal constitution.
Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
It is rationally related to the state’s interest in protecting minor patients seeking mental health care from obtaining ineffective and harmful therapeutic modalities. Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
It is also rationally related to the state’s interest in ensuring its licensed mental health professionals comply with the prevailing standard of care in their field. Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
Subsection (1)(t)(V) does not violate the free exercise clause of the first amendment to the federal constitution.
Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
It targets therapeutic practices because of their harmful effect on minors, rather than their religious nature, and, therefore, is rationally related to the state’s interest. Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
It is also generally applicable to all licensed mental health professionals, regardless of their religious beliefs or affiliations, and there is no mechanism for the regulatory boards to consider the particular reasons for a person’s conduct and determine that providing conversion therapy to children in a professional setting might be permissible. Chiles v. Salazar, 116 F.4th 1178 (10th Cir. 2024).
